/* ---------- 2.1.6 Remedies ---------- */
TOPICS.push({
  id: '2.1.6', unit: 'CT', ref: '2.1.6', title: 'Remedies for breach of contract',
  short: 'Compensatory damages, causation, remoteness, mitigation; rescission, specific performance, rectification, injunctions',
  summary: 'The main remedy for breach of contract is damages, available as of right, to put the claimant in the position they would have been in had the contract been performed. Recoverable losses are limited by causation, remoteness and the duty to mitigate. Equitable remedies — specific performance, injunctions, rescission and rectification — are discretionary and granted where damages are inadequate.',
  spec: ['Common law damages: compensatory damages; the expectation, reliance and restitution interests; non-pecuniary loss', 'Tests of causation', 'Remoteness of damage: Hadley v Baxendale and later cases', 'Mitigation of loss', 'Liquidated damages and penalties', 'Equitable remedies: rescission, specific performance, rectification of documents, injunctions'],
  learn: [
    { h: 'Compensatory damages', html: `
<p>Damages in contract aim to put the claimant, so far as money can, in the position they would have been in had the contract been <b>performed</b> ([[c:robinsonharman]]) — the <b>expectation interest</b>. They are not meant to punish.</p>
<ul><li><b>Expectation loss</b>: difference in value (e.g. the extra cost of buying substitute goods), loss of profit, or cost of cure — but not where cure is unreasonable ([[c:ruxley]]).</li>
<li><b>Reliance loss</b>: wasted expenditure, where profits are too speculative ([[c:anglia]]).</li>
<li><b>Restitution</b>: recovering money paid where there is a total failure of consideration.</li>
<li><b>Nominal damages</b> where there is a breach but no loss.</li>
<li><b>Non-pecuniary loss</b>: generally no damages for distress (<i>Addis v Gramophone</i>, 1909), except where the object of the contract was pleasure, relaxation or peace of mind ([[c:jarvis]]; [[c:farley]]), or for physical inconvenience, or loss of amenity ([[c:ruxley]]).</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'Causation', html: `
<p>The breach must be an <b>effective cause</b> of the loss — the “but for” test is a starting point, but the court asks whether the breach was the effective or dominant cause, applying common sense. An intervening act by the claimant or a third party may break the chain: in <i>Quinn v Burch Bros</i> (1966) a contractor’s failure to supply a stepladder did not cause the injury when the claimant chose to use an unfooted trestle instead. The claimant’s own conduct may reduce damages.</p>` },
    { h: 'Remoteness', html: `
<div class="box def"><b class="lbl">Hadley v Baxendale (1854)</b><p>Damages are recoverable for losses that either (1) arise <b>naturally</b>, in the usual course of things, from the breach; or (2) may reasonably be supposed to have been in the <b>contemplation of both parties</b>, when they made the contract, as the probable result of the breach (because of special knowledge).</p></div>
<ul><li>[[c:hadley]]: lost profits too remote — the carrier did not know the mill had no spare shaft.</li>
<li>[[c:victorialaundry]]: ordinary lost profits recoverable; exceptionally lucrative contracts not.</li>
<li>[[c:heron]]: loss must be “not unlikely” — a higher degree of probability than reasonable foreseeability in tort.</li>
<li>[[c:parsons]]: if the <b>type</b> of loss was contemplated, the full extent is recoverable.</li>
<li>[[c:achilleas]]: the defendant is liable only for losses it can be taken to have <b>assumed responsibility</b> for.</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'Mitigation and agreed damages', html: `
<p><b>Mitigation</b>: the claimant must take reasonable steps to reduce the loss and cannot recover for loss that could have been avoided ([[c:brace]]; <i>British Westinghouse v Underground Electric Railways</i>, 1912). They need not take unreasonable risks or complicated legal action ([[c:pilkington]]). Reasonable costs of mitigating are recoverable. The burden is on the defendant to prove failure to mitigate.</p>
<p><b>Liquidated damages</b>: a clause fixing the amount payable on breach is enforceable unless it is a <b>penalty</b> — a detriment out of all proportion to any legitimate interest of the innocent party in performance ([[c:parkingeye]], replacing the “genuine pre-estimate of loss” test from <i>Dunlop v New Garage</i>, 1915).</p>` },
    { h: 'Equitable remedies', html: `
<p>Equitable remedies are <b>discretionary</b>: granted only where damages would be inadequate, and subject to equitable maxims (“clean hands”, “delay defeats equity”, no hardship to the defendant, no constant supervision).</p>
<ul><li><b>Specific performance</b> — an order to carry out the contract. Common for land and unique goods (paintings, rare items); granted in [[c:beswick]]. Refused for personal service contracts (<i>Ryan v Mutual Tontine</i>, 1893), where constant supervision is needed ([[c:argyll]]), or where the claimant has acted unfairly.</li>
<li><b>Injunctions</b> — an order not to do something (prohibitory) or to do something (mandatory). Negative promises can be enforced ([[c:warnernelson]]; <i>Lumley v Wagner</i>, 1852), but not if it would in effect force someone to work for the claimant ([[c:pageone]]).</li>
<li><b>Rescission</b> — setting aside a voidable contract (misrepresentation, duress), restoring the parties to their original positions. Barred by affirmation, delay, third-party rights and impossibility of restitution (topic 2.1.4).</li>
<li><b>Rectification</b> — correcting a written document that does not record what the parties actually agreed ([[c:craddock]]). Needs convincing proof of a common intention that continued up to signing (<i>Joscelyne v Nissen</i>, 1970).</li></ul>
<p>Other remedies: an action for the <b>agreed sum</b> (the price) and <b>quantum meruit</b> (a reasonable sum for work done).</p>` }
  ],
  debate: [{ q: 'Should damages for disappointment and distress be more widely available?', for: ['Many consumer contracts are about enjoyment, not profit (Jarvis, Farley).', 'Ruxley recognised loss of amenity — the “consumer surplus”.', 'Otherwise consumers are under-compensated when their expectations are ruined.'], ag: ['Commercial contracts should compensate financial loss only (Addis).', 'Hard to value and could encourage trivial claims.', 'Certainty and insurance pricing depend on predictable damages.'] }],
  cases: ['robinsonharman', 'anglia', 'ruxley', 'jarvis', 'farley', 'hadley', 'victorialaundry', 'heron', 'parsons', 'achilleas', 'brace', 'pilkington', 'parkingeye', 'beswick', 'argyll', 'warnernelson', 'pageone', 'craddock'],
  worked: [{ q: '<b>Scenario.</b> Yara runs a bakery. Her new oven, due on 1 March, arrives five weeks late. She loses £3,000 of normal profits, and a £20,000 contract to supply a supermarket that the oven supplier did not know about. She also refuses the supplier’s offer to lend her an oven in the meantime. Advise Yara on damages.', s: ['<b class="st">Aim</b>Expectation damages: put Yara where she would have been had the oven arrived on time (Robinson v Harman).', '<b class="st">Remoteness</b>Normal lost profits arise naturally — first limb of Hadley v Baxendale, recoverable (Victoria Laundry). The supermarket contract was not known to the supplier — second limb not satisfied, too remote.', '<b class="st">Mitigation</b>Refusing a reasonable offer of a loan oven may be a failure to mitigate (Brace v Calder). Losses she could have avoided after the offer are not recoverable.', '<b class="st">Conclude</b>She can recover lost normal profits up to the point she could have mitigated; not the supermarket contract.'], a: 'Normal lost profits (reduced for failure to mitigate); supermarket loss too remote.' }],
  pitfalls: ['Using the tort remoteness test (Wagon Mound) in contract — use Hadley v Baxendale and The Heron II.', 'Forgetting that equitable remedies are discretionary.', 'Saying specific performance is available for employment contracts.', 'Ignoring mitigation in a problem question when the facts hint at it.'],
  cards: [
    ['Aim of contract damages?', 'Put the claimant in the position as if the contract had been performed (Robinson v Harman).'],
    ['Reliance loss — case?', 'Anglia TV v Reed (1972): wasted expenditure.'],
    ['Two limbs of Hadley v Baxendale?', 'Losses arising naturally; losses in the reasonable contemplation of both parties.'],
    ['Victoria Laundry (1949)?', 'Normal profits recoverable; lucrative special contracts not.'],
    ['The Heron II (1969)?', 'Loss must be “not unlikely” — stricter than tort.'],
    ['Parsons v Uttley Ingham (1978)?', 'If the type of loss is contemplated, full extent recoverable.'],
    ['The Achilleas (2008)?', 'Liability limited to losses the defendant assumed responsibility for.'],
    ['Jarvis v Swans Tours (1973)?', 'Damages for disappointment on a ruined holiday.'],
    ['Ruxley v Forsyth (1996)?', 'Cost of cure disproportionate; loss of amenity damages.'],
    ['Duty to mitigate — case?', 'Brace v Calder (1895).'],
    ['Penalty test after ParkingEye v Beavis?', 'Out of all proportion to the innocent party’s legitimate interest.'],
    ['When is specific performance refused?', 'Damages adequate, personal services, constant supervision (Argyll), unclean hands, hardship.'],
    ['Warner Bros v Nelson (1937)?', 'Injunction enforcing a negative promise not to work for rivals.'],
    ['Rectification?', 'Correcting a written document to reflect the true agreement — Craddock v Hunt.']
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Contract damages usually aim to protect the…', o: ['expectation interest', 'reliance interest only', 'defendant’s interest', 'public interest'], x: 'Robinson v Harman.' },
    { q: 'In Hadley v Baxendale the lost profits were too remote because…', o: ['the carrier did not know the mill had no spare shaft', 'the shaft was delivered on time', 'the mill was not open', 'the loss was nominal'], x: 'Second limb not satisfied.' },
    { q: 'Which case allowed damages for a disappointing holiday?', o: ['Jarvis v Swans Tours', 'Addis v Gramophone', 'Brace v Calder', 'Hadley v Baxendale'], x: 'Pleasure was the object.' },
    { q: 'The duty to mitigate means the claimant must…', o: ['take reasonable steps to reduce their loss', 'accept any offer', 'sue quickly', 'reduce the price'], x: 'Brace v Calder.' },
    { q: 'In Ruxley Electronics v Forsyth the claimant received…', o: ['£2,500 for loss of amenity', 'the full cost of rebuilding the pool', 'nothing', 'specific performance'], x: 'Cost of cure unreasonable.' },
    { q: 'Specific performance was refused in Co-op Insurance v Argyll Stores because…', o: ['it would require constant supervision', 'damages were unavailable', 'the contract was for land', 'the claimant delayed'], x: 'Keep-open covenant.' },
    { q: 'An injunction was refused in Page One Records v Britton because it would…', o: ['in effect force the Troggs to keep their manager', 'be too expensive', 'breach privity', 'be a penalty'], x: 'Indirect specific performance of personal service.' },
    { q: 'According to ParkingEye v Beavis, the £85 charge was…', o: ['not a penalty — it protected a legitimate interest', 'a penalty', 'an exclusion clause', 'unfair under s62'], x: 'Managing parking spaces.' },
    { q: 'The Heron II required the loss to be…', o: ['“not unlikely” to result', 'reasonably foreseeable', 'certain', 'caused deliberately'], x: 'Higher than the tort test.' },
    { q: 'Which is NOT an equitable remedy?', o: ['Compensatory damages', 'Specific performance', 'Rectification', 'Injunction'], x: 'Damages are a common law remedy available as of right.' }
  ],
  exam: [
    { q: 'Explain the rules on remoteness of damage in contract. [10]', m: 10, ms: ['purpose — limit liability', 'Hadley v Baxendale — two limbs', 'Victoria Laundry — normal v special profits', 'The Heron II — not unlikely; stricter than tort', 'Parsons — type of loss', 'The Achilleas — assumption of responsibility', 'knowledge at time of contracting', 'contrast with tort (Wagon Mound)'] },
    { q: 'Analyse and evaluate the effectiveness of remedies for breach of contract. [25]', m: 25, ms: ['damages as of right — expectation (Robinson v Harman)', 'reliance/restitution alternatives', 'limits — causation, remoteness, mitigation', 'non-pecuniary loss — Addis v Jarvis/Farley/Ruxley', 'penalties — ParkingEye', 'specific performance — discretion; limits (Argyll)', 'injunctions — Warner Bros v Page One', 'rescission bars; rectification', 'cost of litigation / consumer remedies under CRA', 'conclusion'] }
  ],
  tools: ['remedysort', 'remotetree']
});

